    "payload": {
      "content": "When reviewing papers that propose novel impurity measures or ensemble strategies (e.g., using compression complexity like ETC instead of Gini/Entropy), the SIGN dimension requires rigorous scrutiny of performance relative to strong baselines like Random Forest. A key weakness to identify is if the method fails to demonstrate state-of-the-art performance or shows significant degradation on standard benchmarks (SIGN-SOT). Additionally, if the paper claims domain-specific benefits (e.g., handling temporal or ordered data) but lacks experimental evidence on such datasets, this constitutes a weakness in Domain Impact (SIGN-DOM). Reviewers should also check for the absence of computational cost analysis, which is critical for Real-World Impact (SIGN-IMP), as novel measures often incur higher overhead than standard entropy calculations. The conceptual novelty (SIGN-BRD) is insufficient without proof of practical utility or theoretical justification for non-i.i.d. scenarios.",
